explanation:
System prompt: 'You are a research assistant. Search for [topic], evaluate results, read the 3 most relevant pages. Write a report with: title, 3-paragraph summary, 5 key findings (each cited), and source list. Stop after reading 3 pages.'

Tools: web_search({query}) → [{title, url, snippet}], read_url({url}) → string (truncated), write_report({title, summary, findings, sources}) → {success, filename}

Key details: web_search returns snippets so the agent can evaluate relevance BEFORE reading the full page (saves tokens).

```js
function shouldSearchFirst(question) {
  const volatilitySignals = /current|latest|as of|today|this (week|month|year)|price|revenue|headcount|users?|version|release/i;
  const isStableFact = /atomic (number|mass)|boiling point|definition of|formula for/i.test(question);

  if (isStableFact) return false;          // stable, textbook-style facts: answer directly
  if (volatilitySignals.test(question)) return true;  // named as time-sensitive
  return true; // default to searching when unsure — a wasted search is cheap, a stale confident answer isn't
}
```
The key design decision is the default: when the rule can't confidently classify a question, it should search rather than guess. This is a cheap-mistake-versus-expensive-mistake tradeoff — an unnecessary search costs a few seconds and a fraction of a cent; a confidently wrong answer costs the user's trust.

```js
async function readStage(candidate, question) {
  let pageText;
  try {
    const res = await fetch(candidate.url, { timeout: 8000 });
    if (!res.ok) return { url: candidate.url, extracted: null, error: `fetch failed: ${res.status}` };
    pageText = await extractReadableText(await res.text()); // strip nav/ads/boilerplate
  } catch (err) {
    return { url: candidate.url, extracted: null, error: err.message };
  }

  const extraction = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',
    max_tokens: 400,
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content:
      `Question: ${question}\n\nExtract only the passage(s) from this page that are relevant to the question. Quote them directly. If nothing on the page is relevant, say 'NOT_RELEVANT'.\n\nPage:\n${pageText.slice(0, 6000)}` }]
  });
  const extracted = extraction.content[0].text;
  return {
    url: candidate.url,
    extracted: extracted.includes('NOT_RELEVANT') ? null : extracted,
  };
}
```
The function always returns something with the URL attached — a failed fetch or an irrelevant page isn't silently dropped, it's reported as null so the search/stopping stage can decide whether to try another candidate.

```js
function hasEnoughEvidence(findings, { minIndependentSources = 2, maxSearches = 8, stakes = 'normal' } = {}) {
  const required = stakes === 'high' ? minIndependentSources + 1 : minIndependentSources;

  const independentSources = dedupeByOriginalSource(findings); // collapse republished/syndicated copies
  const agreeing = independentSources.filter(f => f.agreesWithMajority);

  if (findings.length >= maxSearches) return { stop: true, reason: 'search cap reached' };
  if (agreeing.length >= required && independentSources.length >= required) {
    return { stop: true, reason: `${agreeing.length} independent sources agree` };
  }
  return { stop: false, reason: 'insufficient independent agreement so far' };
}
```
The function stops either on genuine convergence (enough independent sources agreeing, scaled up for higher-stakes questions) or on the hard cap — never on an arbitrary fixed number of searches regardless of what they found. `dedupeByOriginalSource` matters because counting five copies of the same wire article as five confirmations is exactly the illusion this module's fifth lesson names directly.

```js
function normalize(text) {
  return text.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, ' ').replace(/["\u2018\u2019\u201c\u201d]/g, "'").trim();
}

function citationIsValid(claim, sourceQuote, fetchedPageText) {
  const normalizedQuote = normalize(sourceQuote);
  const normalizedPage = normalize(fetchedPageText);
  const found = normalizedPage.includes(normalizedQuote);
  return {
    valid: found,
    reason: found
      ? 'quote located in fetched source text'
      : 'quote does not appear in the source page — citation may be fabricated or paraphrased past recognition',
  };
}
```
This checks the actual mechanism of a fabricated citation: a URL that exists but doesn't say what the claim says it says. Normalizing whitespace and quote characters avoids false negatives from trivial formatting differences while still catching a quote that genuinely isn't there. Run this on every citation before the answer ships, not spot-checked after the fact.

```js
async function applyAndVerify(change, testCommand) {
  await applyChange(change);
  const result = await runShell(testCommand); // the FULL suite, not a scoped subset
  if (result.exitCode === 0) {
    return { passed: true, output: result.stdout };
  }
  return {
    passed: false,
    output: result.stdout + result.stderr,
    failedTests: parseFailedTestNames(result.stdout),
    guidance: 'Do not retry the same change blindly — read the failure output above and diagnose the actual cause before the next attempt.',
  };
}
```
The function deliberately returns rich failure detail instead of a bare boolean, and it runs the complete suite every time — a change that only ever gets checked against the test it was meant to fix can pass locally while silently breaking something the agent never looked at.

```js
async function processBatch(items, processFn, { concurrency = 8, maxRetries = 3 } = {}) {
  const results = [];
  let index = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (index < items.length) {
      const i = index++;
      const item = items[i];
      let attempt = 0;
      while (true) {
        try {
          results[i] = { item, result: await processFn(item), error: null };
          break;
        } catch (err) {
          if (err.status === 429 && attempt < maxRetries) {
            attempt++;
            await sleep(2 ** attempt * 1000); // exponential backoff
            continue;
          }
          results[i] = { item, result: null, error: err.message }; // non-retryable, record and move on
          break;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: concurrency }, worker));
  return results;
}
```
Bounded concurrency (a handful of workers pulling from a shared queue) keeps throughput reasonable without hitting rate limits the way full parallelism would. Backoff-and-retry applies specifically to rate-limit errors (429) — a malformed item or a genuine processing error gets recorded and the batch moves on, rather than retried uselessly.

```js
function validateExtraction(record) {
  const errors = [];
  if (typeof record.name !== 'string' || record.name.trim().length === 0) errors.push('name: missing or empty');
  if (!/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/.test(record.email || '')) errors.push('email: not a valid email shape');
  if (typeof record.total !== 'number' || record.total < 0 || record.total > 100000) errors.push('total: missing, negative, or implausibly large');
  const d = new Date(record.date);
  const now = new Date();
  if (isNaN(d) || d > now || d < new Date('2000-01-01')) errors.push('date: missing, invalid, or outside a plausible range');

  return { valid: errors.length === 0, errors };
}
```
Each check is specific enough to tell a reviewer exactly what to look at rather than just 'this record failed' — a negative total and a malformed email need different fixes, and a human triaging a batch of failures benefits from not having to re-derive which is which.

```js
function buildEscalationPackage(conversation) {
  return {
    userRequest: extractCoreRequest(conversation), // specific, not paraphrased into vagueness
    attemptedSteps: conversation.turns
      .filter(t => t.role === 'assistant' && t.action)
      .map(t => ({ action: t.action, outcome: t.outcome })),
    unresolvedBecause: conversation.escalationReason, // e.g. 'requires manager-level refund authorization'
    relevantContext: {
      accountId: conversation.accountId,
      orderIds: extractOrderReferences(conversation),
    },
    // deliberately NOT included: a generic mood label like 'frustrated' with nothing concrete behind it
  };
}
```
The package is built around specifics a human can act on immediately — what was tried, what's blocking resolution, and the concrete references (order IDs, account) needed to pick up the thread without asking the user to restate everything.
